WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:09.130
What's up, Wisecrack, Jared again.

00:00:09.130 --> 00:00:14.200
Now, you would think that after almost 20
years of some of the most intense fan hatred

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:15.710
ever, "Thumbs way down!"

00:00:15.710 --> 00:00:16.990
"You didn't like it?"

00:00:16.990 --> 00:00:21.160
"No, I think I deserve a public apology from
George Lucas," there’d be nothing new to

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:24.750
say about the worst Star War, The Phantom
Menace.

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:26.750
And yet, here we are.

00:00:26.750 --> 00:00:31.490
After the release of Return of the Jedi in
1983, millions of fans begged George Lucas

00:00:31.490 --> 00:00:33.500
to create a new Star Wars film.

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:35.440
Studio executives begged him.

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:36.460
Toymakers begged him.

00:00:36.460 --> 00:00:42.090
And after constantly saying no, finally, in
1999, everyone got their wish.

00:00:42.090 --> 00:00:44.710
George Lucas released The Phantom Menace.

00:00:44.710 --> 00:00:46.270
Problem solved.

00:00:46.270 --> 00:00:47.980
Except, not really.

00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:52.660
Upon release, Star Wars fans rose up in fury
and demanded the head of George Lucas on a

00:00:52.660 --> 00:00:54.050
Tusken spike.

00:00:54.050 --> 00:00:58.150
They rallied together in AOL chat rooms and
Yahoo message boards and for the first time

00:00:58.150 --> 00:01:02.341
in the short history of the internet - fans
from across the world banded together in the

00:01:02.341 --> 00:01:07.140
righteous cause of tearing George Lucas a
new one for the unforgivable sin that was

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:08.530
making this movie.

00:01:08.530 --> 00:01:10.100
And hey, look... they had a point.

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:13.329
The Phantom Menace, for lack of a better word,
sucks.

00:01:13.329 --> 00:01:18.030
Hundreds of articles, essays, and videos have
been written about how it sucks (shout-out

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:24.570
Mr. Plinkett), "So, you might like the characters,
you know, if you're stupid," and why it sucks.

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:29.400
So much so, that it has become a given that
there is absolutely nothing redeemable about

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:30.400
this movie.

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:33.049
But what if that’s not entirely true?

00:01:33.049 --> 00:01:36.430
Relax, I’m not going to argue that midichlorians
were a great idea.

00:01:36.430 --> 00:01:38.889
"It is possible he was conceived by midichlorians."

00:01:38.889 --> 00:01:43.369
Instead, I want to talk about what The Phantom
Menace gets almost right.

00:01:43.369 --> 00:01:47.749
Because conceptually, there are some very
promising themes at play, here.

00:01:47.749 --> 00:01:48.749
Namely: Nazis.

00:01:48.749 --> 00:01:52.939
It’s no secret that the original trilogy
largely wanted you to conflate The Empire

00:01:52.939 --> 00:01:57.530
with the Third Reich; from the costumes, to
the use of color, and of course, naming the

00:01:57.530 --> 00:01:58.689
soldiers stormtroopers.

00:01:58.689 --> 00:02:02.270
But how did the galaxy get to be this way?

00:02:02.270 --> 00:02:08.170
Like Nazi Germany, which descended into totalitarianism
through democratic means, George Lucas aspired

00:02:08.170 --> 00:02:13.760
to portray something immensely important with
the prequels: how a peaceful republic can

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:17.430
democratically descend into full-blown Naziism.

00:02:17.430 --> 00:02:18.860
So, what went wrong?

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:20.250
Well, let’s find out.

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:25.040
Welcome to this Wisecrack Editionon Star Wars:
Episode I - The Phantom Menace: What Went

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:26.040
Wrong?

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:28.129
And uhh... can you spoil a movie with no plot?

00:02:28.129 --> 00:02:32.319
Oh, and if you wanna hear us nerd out about
the Disney Star Wars backlash, check out our

00:02:32.319 --> 00:02:35.560
special episode on our movie podcast Show
Me The Meaning.

00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:38.160
You will smash that link in the description.

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:42.079
For everyone who hasn’t seen this in over
a decade: Here’s a brief recap.

00:02:42.079 --> 00:02:47.549
An organization called the trade federation
is protesting trade taxes by... blockading

00:02:47.549 --> 00:02:48.780
trade with the planet Naboo.

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:52.500
But I guess it doesn’t need to make sense,
because really they’re doing it all for

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:55.879
their boy, Darth Sidious, AKA Senator Palpatine.

00:02:55.879 --> 00:03:00.269
The Republic sends a certain Qui-Gon Jinn
and his apprentice Obi-Wan Kenobi to negotiate,

00:03:00.269 --> 00:03:03.170
but the trade federation tries to kill them.

00:03:03.170 --> 00:03:07.569
They flee to the surface of Naboo, which is
now being invaded by the Trade Federation,

00:03:07.569 --> 00:03:11.870
encounter the worst character ever conceived
in cinematic history, —&nbsp;"Ex-squeeze me!"

00:03:11.870 --> 00:03:15.280
— save the queen, and try to zoom off to
the Galactic Senate where they can explain

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:18.120
what a bunch of jerks the trade federation
is.

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:22.769
But they get shot up and have to find supplies
on Tatooine, where they meet a nice slave-boy

00:03:22.769 --> 00:03:24.590
with incredible force-blood.

00:03:24.590 --> 00:03:29.060
There’s some light gambling, they liberate
the boy, leave his mother to rot, and head

00:03:29.060 --> 00:03:30.760
to the senate to plead their case.

00:03:30.760 --> 00:03:35.799
There’s a battle, Qui-Gon dies, and Obi-Wan
murders Darth Maul, and Palpatine is now the

00:03:35.799 --> 00:03:38.020
head honcho of the Galactic Senate.

00:03:38.020 --> 00:03:40.420
Also, Jar Jar is a general.

00:03:40.420 --> 00:03:41.450
"General!"

00:03:41.450 --> 00:03:42.480
Hmm.

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:47.890
The original trilogy, while wonderfully enjoyable,
are not complex works of emotional art.

00:03:47.890 --> 00:03:49.260
They’re not intended to be.

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:53.459
They are updates of the Flash Gordon and Buck
Rogers serials from Lucas’s childhood.

00:03:53.459 --> 00:03:58.290
The heroes were good men who often wore white
and the villains were evil men who often wore

00:03:58.290 --> 00:04:01.180
black, just like Luke Skywalker and Darth
Vader.

00:04:01.180 --> 00:04:04.850
There is no ambiguity here, and that’s OK.

00:04:04.850 --> 00:04:08.170
The Phantom Menace, on the other hand, is
quite ambiguous.

00:04:08.170 --> 00:04:11.510
Qui-Gon lies and cheats his way through most
of the story.

00:04:11.510 --> 00:04:16.770
He, as per the Light Side of the Force, quietly
meditates before his final confrontation against

00:04:16.770 --> 00:04:20.530
Darth Maul... and gets REKT.

00:04:20.530 --> 00:04:27.350
Obi-Wan fills with rage, an emotion associated
with the Dark Side, and only then can he effectively

00:04:27.350 --> 00:04:29.030
avenge his master.

00:04:29.030 --> 00:04:32.200
Not quite the black-and-white morality of
the first three films.

00:04:32.200 --> 00:04:35.870
But most notable is our benevolent heroine,
Queen Amidala.

00:04:35.870 --> 00:04:39.120
She spends The Phantom Menace trying to save
her people.

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:43.730
But for all her noble intentions, she mostly
gets manipulated by Senator Palpatine and

00:04:43.730 --> 00:04:46.880
even proves integral in getting him elected
Supreme Chancellor.

00:04:46.880 --> 00:04:52.720
When good leads to victory for evil, we’ve
entered the realm of the morally ambiguous.

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:57.060
In his essay “Art and Moral Education,"
philosopher Christopher Hamilton expounds

00:04:57.060 --> 00:05:02.260
on the richness of such moral ambiguity insomuch
as they force us to confront our own moral

00:05:02.260 --> 00:05:03.260
beliefs.

00:05:03.260 --> 00:05:05.120
It’s a sign of great art.

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:09.500
So, did the Phantom Menace give us that gut
punch that comes with us re-evaluating our

00:05:09.500 --> 00:05:11.320
own moral beliefs?

00:05:11.320 --> 00:05:13.500
Of course it did, right!

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:14.500
Right???

00:05:14.500 --> 00:05:15.500
Okay, just kidding.

00:05:15.500 --> 00:05:18.560
I felt nothing all 20 times I’ve seen this
movie.

00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:24.050
Ambiguity is a great idea when dealing with
complex political systems, trade federations,

00:05:24.050 --> 00:05:26.740
planetary autonomy, solving humanitarian crises.

00:05:26.740 --> 00:05:29.330
So, why didn’t it work?

00:05:29.330 --> 00:05:31.860
Because the characters have no agency.

00:05:31.860 --> 00:05:37.009
Author Chuck Wendig, who has written extensively
on the subject, defines agency in fiction

00:05:37.009 --> 00:05:41.960
as “a demonstration of the character’s
ability to make decisions and affect the story.

00:05:41.960 --> 00:05:44.740
[They have] motivations all [their] own.

00:05:44.740 --> 00:05:47.560
[They are] active more than [they are] reactive.

00:05:47.560 --> 00:05:51.240
[They push] the plot more than the plot pushes
on [them].”

00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:55.990
In contrast, he writes that “characters
without agency tend to be like little paper

00:05:55.990 --> 00:05:58.099
boats bobbing down a river of your own making.

00:05:58.099 --> 00:05:59.310
They cannot steer.

00:05:59.310 --> 00:06:01.360
They cannot change the course of the river.

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:05.449
The river is an external force that carries
them along — meaning, the plot sticks its

00:06:05.449 --> 00:06:09.880
hand up the character’s cavernous bottom-hole
and makes the character do things and say

00:06:09.880 --> 00:06:12.349
things in service to the plot.”

00:06:12.349 --> 00:06:16.710
In The Phantom Menace, it’s almost like
Lucas took this rule of bad writing and ran

00:06:16.710 --> 00:06:17.710
with it.

00:06:17.710 --> 00:06:22.740
Qui-Gon, and Anakin, and Padme, and Obi-Wan,
and, God help me, Jar-Jar Binks are nothing

00:06:22.740 --> 00:06:24.690
but little paper boats.

00:06:24.690 --> 00:06:29.979
They have no internal conflict, no external
life, no personal obsession outside of what

00:06:29.979 --> 00:06:31.440
the film dictates.

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:33.440
And that river pushing them along?

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:34.770
It’s the darkside.

00:06:34.770 --> 00:06:40.720
"The dark side clouds everything.

00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:44.479
Impossible to see, the future is."

00:06:44.479 --> 00:06:49.449
And the biggest casualty, besides the time
you just wasted being bored, is the political

00:06:49.449 --> 00:06:50.860
message itself.

00:06:50.860 --> 00:06:53.680
The best example of this is the story of Queen
Amidala.

00:06:53.680 --> 00:06:58.190
Padme, along with many of the other characters,
are mere pawns of Palpatine.

00:06:58.190 --> 00:07:00.490
That’s not necessarily a bad thing.

00:07:00.490 --> 00:07:05.160
But as we’ll see, Palpatine’s manipulations
quickly turn into “How To Cover Up Bad Writing

00:07:05.160 --> 00:07:06.160
101.”

00:07:06.160 --> 00:07:09.520
Here’s what we know about Padme: She’s
a queen, who doesn’t want to submit to some

00:07:09.520 --> 00:07:11.380
invader, because that’s what queens do.

00:07:11.380 --> 00:07:14.889
She also likes democracy, because, hey, she’s
not evil.

00:07:14.889 --> 00:07:20.419
So, in Star Wars, how does a queen who likes
democracy lead to the eventual downfall of

00:07:20.419 --> 00:07:21.759
said democracy?

00:07:21.759 --> 00:07:23.400
She gets manipulated.

00:07:23.400 --> 00:07:28.080
Palpatine creates a crisis on Naboo, which
mirrors the prototypical “false flag”

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:32.520
attack of the Reichstag fire, and she has
to plead to the Senate to intervene.

00:07:32.520 --> 00:07:37.639
But Palpatine, since he’s smart, knows they’re
feckless bureaucrats and will do nothing.

00:07:37.639 --> 00:07:42.330
He plants a seed in Amidala’s mind, "I must
be frank, Your Majesty, there is little chance

00:07:42.330 --> 00:07:47.349
the Senate will act on the invasion," she
sees his warning come true, "This is where

00:07:47.349 --> 00:07:51.280
chancellor Valorum's strength will disappear."

00:07:51.280 --> 00:07:54.919
"The point is conceded.

00:07:54.919 --> 00:07:59.819
Will you defer your motion to allow a commission
to investigate the validity of your accusations?"

00:07:59.819 --> 00:08:04.800
And calls for the Supreme Chancellor to be
ousted, "I move for a vote of no confidence

00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:06.400
in chancellor Valorum's leadership."

00:08:06.400 --> 00:08:11.199
So that a real statesman can take charge and
cut through the bureaucratic red tape.

00:08:11.199 --> 00:08:15.419
That real statesman just happens to be our
ambitious senator and future Emperor, Sheev

00:08:15.419 --> 00:08:17.449
Palpatine, aka Darth Sidious.

00:08:17.449 --> 00:08:20.800
Here’s where our paper boats become a problem.

00:08:20.800 --> 00:08:25.169
Characters without agency are, well, boring,
but in this case of trying to get into the

00:08:25.169 --> 00:08:30.789
complexity of not only how democracies fail,
but how well-meaning people are implicated,

00:08:30.789 --> 00:08:35.820
paper boat characters rob us of our ability
to reflect on what’s actually happening.

00:08:35.820 --> 00:08:37.680
Beat for beat, it makes sense.

00:08:37.680 --> 00:08:41.870
Palpatine warns her of something, she sees
his warning come true, so she falls for the

00:08:41.870 --> 00:08:42.870
deception.

00:08:42.870 --> 00:08:44.269
It even sounds good.

00:08:44.269 --> 00:08:49.480
But maybe because Lucas was too busy cramming
in 8,000 dumb lines for this guy, it falls

00:08:49.480 --> 00:08:50.750
flat.

00:08:50.750 --> 00:08:53.540
Each beat could have confronted our character
with a choice.

00:08:53.540 --> 00:08:58.090
They might deliberate over, struggle with,
or at least talk about this choice.

00:08:58.090 --> 00:09:01.991
She might argue with Palpatine, she might
confide in a friend, she might reach out to

00:09:01.991 --> 00:09:06.209
an outside government for help, she might
deny Palpatine’s bargain at all costs until

00:09:06.209 --> 00:09:08.959
the toll on her citizenry gets too high.

00:09:08.959 --> 00:09:12.040
But in the Phantom Menace, things just kind
of happen.

00:09:12.040 --> 00:09:16.330
Because everyone is a pawn, not a person with
agency.

00:09:16.330 --> 00:09:20.380
And that destroys our ability, as the audience,
to reflect on these issues.

00:09:20.380 --> 00:09:25.550
When we identify with the characters, we identify
with their dilemma, asking ourselves, “what

00:09:25.550 --> 00:09:26.690
would I do?”

00:09:26.690 --> 00:09:30.890
When, in the Dark Knight, the Joker rigs two
boats with explosives to give each one the

00:09:30.890 --> 00:09:35.260
power to obliterate the other before they
get obliterated themselves, "At midnight,

00:09:35.260 --> 00:09:38.329
I blow you all up.

00:09:38.329 --> 00:09:43.040
If, however, one of you presses the button,
I'll let that boat live," we bite our nails

00:09:43.040 --> 00:09:46.950
as we debate the merits of killing someone
else to save our own skin.

00:09:46.950 --> 00:09:52.180
There's no such tension in The Phantom Menace
because Padme is just Palpatine’s puppet.

00:09:52.180 --> 00:09:54.050
And what’s the deal with the other senators?

00:09:54.050 --> 00:09:55.050
What are their motivations?

00:09:55.050 --> 00:09:59.300
Who knows, we’re just told when, and how,
they side with Palpatine.

00:09:59.300 --> 00:10:04.090
And to make matters worse, at no point does
Padme regret the decision that would eventually

00:10:04.090 --> 00:10:07.820
destroy democracy, in the Phantom Menace or
in the later movies.

00:10:07.820 --> 00:10:12.320
If her initial decision to remove the Supreme
Chancellor from power gives us no room to

00:10:12.320 --> 00:10:16.380
empathize and think, at least we can empathize
with and think about her regret.

00:10:16.380 --> 00:10:17.380
But no.

00:10:17.380 --> 00:10:24.639
For a woman who later says this: "So this
is how liberty dies, with thunderous applause..."

00:10:24.639 --> 00:10:29.550
You might at least expect her to sob quietly
in some corner because she literally made

00:10:29.550 --> 00:10:30.550
this happen.

00:10:30.550 --> 00:10:33.420
By contrast, consider Jaime Lannister from
Game of Thrones.

00:10:33.420 --> 00:10:35.800
He was a loyal guard to the Mad King.

00:10:35.800 --> 00:10:40.370
Except, when the Mad King tried to incinerate
all of his citizens, "Burn them all!"

00:10:40.370 --> 00:10:44.389
Jaime broke his sacred oath and murdered the
person he swore to protect.

00:10:44.389 --> 00:10:50.240
"When the king turned to flee, I drove my
sword into his back."

00:10:50.240 --> 00:10:54.910
Jaime is haunted by this decision as he’s
derisively called "Kingslayer" for the rest

00:10:54.910 --> 00:10:59.540
of his life, and while he probably made the
right choice, he is also acutely aware that

00:10:59.540 --> 00:11:03.930
he is more or less responsible for the current
sh*tshow in Westeros.

00:11:03.930 --> 00:11:08.870
He takes responsibility for the choices he
made, and that allows us to identify with

00:11:08.870 --> 00:11:09.870
him.

00:11:09.870 --> 00:11:13.930
Or we can even look at Rogue One, where a
good character’s involvement in the creation

00:11:13.930 --> 00:11:16.610
of the Death Star is put front and center.

00:11:16.610 --> 00:11:20.760
Not only does this lack of agency rob meaning
from the “how democracy dies” theme in

00:11:20.760 --> 00:11:25.270
the Phantom Menace, but it also makes all
of Lucas’ topical and timely takes on news

00:11:25.270 --> 00:11:28.200
of the decade more or less meaningless.

00:11:28.200 --> 00:11:32.810
The Phantom Menace came out in the late 90s,
just years after governments in Serbia and

00:11:32.810 --> 00:11:34.470
Rwanda had committed genocide.

00:11:34.470 --> 00:11:39.520
So, when the Queen of Naboo is pleading to
an international, er, interplanetary government

00:11:39.520 --> 00:11:44.240
body to intervene in the slaughter of her
people, George Lucas’s inspiration was pretty

00:11:44.240 --> 00:11:45.240
obvious.

00:11:45.240 --> 00:11:47.339
On a narrative level, do we care?

00:11:47.339 --> 00:11:48.339
Not really.

00:11:48.339 --> 00:11:52.519
Vague shoutouts to current events don’t
a good political allegory make.

00:11:52.519 --> 00:11:53.760
You need choices.

00:11:53.760 --> 00:11:58.829
Instead, we understand it as just another
step in Palpatine’s greater machinations.

00:11:58.829 --> 00:12:03.290
When the senate fails to do anything, we don’t
understand their motivations for doing so,

00:12:03.290 --> 00:12:08.329
or their personal failings, aside from a line
or two from Palpatine describing them as ineffectual.

00:12:08.329 --> 00:12:10.250
And then there’s the trade federation.

00:12:10.250 --> 00:12:14.580
Talk of taxes and trade in the opening crawl
was likely meant to invoke contemporary debates

00:12:14.580 --> 00:12:19.769
about globalization and free trade, right
on the heels of NAFTA which passed in 1994

00:12:19.769 --> 00:12:21.490
and anti-globalization protests.

00:12:21.490 --> 00:12:24.040
So, what does the trade federation want?

00:12:24.040 --> 00:12:26.870
Why did the senate pass taxes on trade?

00:12:26.870 --> 00:12:27.870
Who the f**k knows.

00:12:27.870 --> 00:12:31.810
It’s also worth noting that, aside from
the politic themes, characters getting pushed

00:12:31.810 --> 00:12:35.220
along the narrative stream with no choice
is everywhere.

00:12:35.220 --> 00:12:39.199
In the case of Jar Jar, he literally succeeds
via incompetence.

00:12:39.199 --> 00:12:44.110
In the battle of Naboo, his fumbling and carelessness
accidentally releases whatever these are,

00:12:44.110 --> 00:12:45.940
which destroy a bunch of enemy droids.

00:12:45.940 --> 00:12:49.139
I think this is what business people mean
by "failing up."

00:12:49.139 --> 00:12:52.980
And then there’s Anakin, who pushes a bunch
of wrong buttons, which ferry him to the droid

00:12:52.980 --> 00:12:56.780
ship, where he can press a few more buttons
to save the day.

00:12:56.780 --> 00:12:58.860
"Now this is podracing!"

00:12:58.860 --> 00:13:02.079
So, what if this the real curse of The Phantom
Menace.

00:13:02.079 --> 00:13:04.950
Because it could have been a powerful movie.

00:13:04.950 --> 00:13:08.490
More importantly, it could have been a powerful
children’s movie, and joined the likes of

00:13:08.490 --> 00:13:13.279
Harry Potter as “kids fiction covering horrific
political history.”

00:13:13.279 --> 00:13:14.760
But it’s not.

00:13:14.760 --> 00:13:15.899
Not even close.

00:13:15.899 --> 00:14:03.130
Just mark it down as another example of grandiose
ambitions ending 

00:14:03.130 --> 00:14:26.980
in 
a faceplant.

